#doc (Manual) "Oriented cells" =>

# The Oriented Cell Articulating Generates

The claim-site carried here is:

* claim-site: the oriented predication cell the articulating recursion picks
  out;
* phase: language, predicating out of articulating;
* generating sites: the articulating recursion, the reflexive seed, sibling
  composition;
* Lean carrier: `Obsmetrologia.Language.Predicating.Oriented`;
* Blueprint node: `thm:oriented-cell`;
* provenance: the recursion `pair u a` read as the head articulated over the
  tail;
* familiar comparison: an edge of a graph labelled by a relation; a pair of
  events with "precedes" between them;
* danger: reading the reflexive seed as the whole of predicating. The seed
  is only the floor; `Spine.predicate` is this page's cell, and it is also
  what *measuring* a trace reads (see `Positing.Measure`).

The governing sentence is:

> `pair u a` is the head articulated over the tail. Its oriented cell is the
> head's token, the head's claim, the tail's head token.

## On The Floor, The Reflexive Seed

The spine's predicating morphism reads only the head of an articulating, so
every cell it generates is reflexive. The oriented cell agrees with it on the
floor:

```lean
example (u : Utterance) :
    Articulating.orientedCell (Articulating.utter u) = PredicationCell.seed u :=
  Articulating.orientedCell_utter u
```

## At Depth One, Orientation

Above the floor the oriented cell is the head composed with the utterance
immediately below it. That is the one sibling composition the recursion
determines without a choice from the pool, and it lies in that pool:

```lean
example (u : Utterance) (a : Articulating) :
    Articulating.orientedCell (Articulating.pair u a)
      = PredicationCell.composeSeeds u a.head :=
  Articulating.orientedCell_pair_eq_composeSeeds u a

example (a : Articulating) :
    Articulating.orientedCell a ∈ PredicationCell.cellsOf a :=
  Articulating.orientedCell_mem_cellsOf a

example :
    ∃ a : Articulating,
      (Articulating.orientedCell a).subject ≠ (Articulating.orientedCell a).object :=
  Articulating.orientation_generated
```

## Converse And Projections Stay Generated

The converse of a generated cell is generated by the articulation with the two
tokens exchanged and the claim carried, and the two projections recover the
reflexive seeds of the two ends:

```lean
example (u : Utterance) (a : Articulating) :
    PredicationCell.converse (Articulating.orientedCell (Articulating.pair u a))
      = Articulating.orientedCell
          (Articulating.pair ⟨a.head.token, u.claim⟩ (Articulating.utter u)) :=
  Articulating.converse_orientedCell_pair u a

example (a : Articulating) :
    PredicationCell.subjProj (Articulating.orientedCell a) = PredicationCell.seed a.head :=
  Articulating.subjProj_orientedCell a
```

## What This Page Does Not Say

The oriented cell is the spine's predicate (`Spine.predicate_eq_orientedCell`,
since 2026-09-27), and it is what *measuring* reads. `Obsmetrologia.measure k trace φ` (Book II's measured-reading page)
collects, for a claim `φ`, the head tokens of every articulating in a trace
whose `orientedCell` equals `φ` — so it is articulating's own generated
cells, oriented, that a measured valuation is built against, and this page's
`orientedCell` is exactly the function that trace-measuring reads by.
On the floor the spine's predicate is the reflexive seed of the one utterance
(`orientedCell_utter`), so nothing that articulated a single utterance reads
differently. It does not compute a causal order; it
supplies the cells, pairs of tokens with a claim between them, from which a
composition-generated settling would compute one.
